{% set local_problem %}
<p>Lake Guntersville creates one of the worst environments for fascia boards in North Alabama. The humidity rising from 69,000 acres of lake surface keeps exterior wood constantly damp. When gutters overflow or leak — even occasionally — water soaks into fascia that never gets a chance to dry out.</p>

<p>The result is accelerated rot. Fascia that might last 25 years in drier areas can fail in 10-15 years near the lake. And because rot often starts from the inside, damage can be extensive before it becomes visible from the ground.</p>

<p>We see this constantly on lake properties: homeowners who call for new gutters but discover their fascia is too damaged to support them. Installing new gutters on rotted wood is pointless — they won't stay attached, and the underlying damage will only worsen.</p>

<p>For vacation homes, the problem can be worse. Damage develops while owners are away, and what started as minor rot becomes major structural issues before anyone notices.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set service_explanation %}
<p>We begin with thorough inspection, checking fascia (and soffit if needed) for damage that may not be visible from the ground. Lake homes often have more extensive rot than expected — we find it all before providing estimates.</p>

<p>For damaged sections, we remove existing gutters, cut out rotted wood, and install new fascia boards. For Guntersville's humid environment, we typically recommend PVC composite materials — they cost more than wood but resist moisture far better.</p>

<p>New gutters are then installed on solid, properly prepared fascia. We design the system to prevent the overflow and leakage that caused damage in the first place — protecting your repair investment for years to come.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set why_us %}
<p>We've been repairing fascia on Lake Guntersville homes for over 20 years. We understand how the humid environment accelerates damage and which materials and techniques provide lasting protection.</p>

<p>Your lakefront home deserves proper care. We provide it — with quality work, appropriate materials, and honest assessments of what your property needs.</p>
{% endset %}
